On Mon, 22 Dec 2008, JG Miller wrote:

> There exists here a perverted sort of respect for the way the dev team
> has operated.  The lone wolf attitude, in all its forms, KILLS
> attempts at community organizing and effective action here over and
> over and over.
>
> So, short answer on specifics?  How about we all recognize that the
> dev team has failed us, stop cutting them slack after 5 years, apply
> pressure on them, and integrate the idea that the community needs to
> be serious about creating its own project if it wants a better game.

While I think that your analysis is valid (lone wolf = bad for a project 
of this size, many players seem to be blinded, language discussion is 
mostly about a fata morgana), you fail two see two fundamental problems:


1) "...apply pressure on them..."

How? You can only apply pressure to someone who's communicating in some 
form. Listening is not enough (and nobody knows if developers actually 
read this).
Because you can't apply any pressure, half of your efforts here are in 
vain. Put bluntly: assume that the Devteam is deaf, disconnected, dormant. 
Don't expect any official statements; they won't pass the baton, they 
won't announce retirement. (I think that's a pretty sad state and speaks 
for a certain lack of responsibility.)


2) "...integrate the idea that the community needs to be serious about 
creating its own project if it wants a better game."

This is so vague. The 'community' can't produce a better game because 
there is absolutely no concensus on the shortcomings (are there any?) and 
their priorities.
This is why any further development is either done by someone with a plan 
(see Sporkhack) or by someone without a plan (see SLASH'EM). You can work 
in a group but it is silly to assume that -  after a nice time of 
discussing - the agenda for Nethack 0.4 will emerge. Design involves 
decisions and even if you were going by majorities (as opposed to 
vetoing), you'd lose out on all the radical proposals.


These two shortcomings are paraphrased as 'whining': you complain about 
the devteam without being able to change it; you complain about the lack 
of non-devteam progress without going into any details yourself.

For example, why did you never bring up any game-related proposal? A list 
of features to be added, another list of features to be nerfed or removed? 
Why is there no dicussion (ignited by YOU) about whether Spork is a good 
base to start from?

That you correctly perceive the faults of the devteam and of those lost in 
Babel does not make your 'proposal' any more constructive. I think that 
the best way to improve Nethack is by playing Spork on nao and report 
about it.

David




